.TH SMTCAL 1
.SH NAME
smtcal \- what a few instances of a family new to the model are worth to its predictions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smtcal
[
.B \-k
.I k,k,...
]
[
.I pred.tsv
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I smtcal
reads the predictions
.IR smtfit (1)
wrote on test, from the file named or from the standard input, and asks how
much of their error under the split by family is
the level of a family the model never saw.
For each split, task and family of learners, the first
.I k
instances of every family, in the order of their names, set the family's offset:
the median of what the model missed on them.
The other instances are then judged with the offset added.
With
.I k
zero the offset is none, and the score is that of the predictions as they came.
.PP
The family of an instance is the first folder of its name under the logic.
A task of classes has no offset and is left alone.
.PP
The columns, a line a split, task, family of learners and
.IR k :
the instances judged; the mean and root mean squared error; the Spearman
correlation over them all; and
.BR rho_within ,
the same correlation inside each family of three instances or more, weighed
by their number, which no offset changes and so tells what the features order
in a family never seen.
.PP
The values of
.I k
come as
.B \-k
.IR k,k,... ,
zero by default along with 1, 2, 5, 10, 20 and 50.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.nf
smtcal out/pred.tsv > out/calib.tsv
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR smtfit (1),
.IR smtimp (1)
